    (v_topic_id, 'A 94-year-old female named Virginia with advanced dementia (FAST stage 7c) is admitted to the hospital with a third episode of aspiration pneumonia in the past 4 months. Her brother is highly distressed, noting that the patient is eating very little, and asks about placing a percutaneous endoscopic gastrostomy (PEG) feeding tube. According to the American Geriatrics Society (AGS) guidelines, which of the following is the most appropriate counseling and clinical recommendation?', '["Recommend a temporary nasogastric (NG) tube for 6 months to see if she recovers her baseline cognitive function and swallowing capacity", "Suggest starting total parenteral nutrition (TPN) through a central line as a safer and more effective alternative to tube feeding in advanced dementia", "Advise the family that a PEG tube is legally mandatory once a patient with dementia is unable to meet their daily caloric needs orally", "Recommend immediate PEG tube placement to prevent malnutrition, as feeding tubes have been shown to significantly prolong life and prevent decubitus ulcers in advanced dementia", "Recommend continuing careful hand-feeding and comfort-focused care; explain that PEG tube placement in advanced dementia does not prolong survival, reduce the risk of aspiration pneumonia, improve nutritional parameters, or enhance patient comfort"]'::jsonb, 4, 'For patients with advanced dementia (FAST stage 7), clinical evidence demonstrates that **percutaneous endoscopic gastrostomy (PEG) feeding tubes do not prolong survival, reduce aspiration risk, improve nutritional status, or enhance comfort**. Feeding tubes are associated with significant complications, including local infection, tube migration, diarrhea, abdominal bloating, and increased use of physical/chemical restraints to prevent the patient from pulling the tube. Instead, **careful hand-feeding (assisted oral feeding)** should be continued, focusing on patient comfort and flavor preferences. Palliative care consultation is highly valuable to support the family. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 82; AGS Feeding Tubes Position Statement; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 80-year-old female named Mildred has recently undergone staging workup for progressive dyspnea and weight loss, revealing metastatic colorectal cancer. The primary care physician needs to deliver this diagnosis. Which of the following best describes the correct clinical application of the ''S'' (Setting) and ''P'' (Perception) steps of the SPIKES protocol for delivering bad news?', '["Arrange a private, quiet setting with comfortable seating, ask the patient who else they would like to have present, and then ask open-ended questions to assess what the patient already knows or suspects about their medical condition before giving any information", "Schedule the meeting in a noisy clinic waiting area to reduce the patient''s anxiety, and ask the patient''s family to step out during the disclosure", "Deliver the diagnosis immediately in the hospital hallway to ensure timely communication, and skip assessing the patient''s understanding to save time", "Use highly technical medical jargon to establish clinical authority, and then ask the patient to repeat the staging classification", "Ensure the patient is alone to prevent family interference, and immediately show the CT scans to explain the tumor size in detail"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The **SPIKES protocol** is a 6-step framework for delivering bad news: (1) **S - Setting**: Arrange a private, quiet environment, sit down, make eye contact, and invite key family members. (2) **P - Perception**: Assess what the patient understands about their illness using open-ended questions (e.g., ''What is your understanding of why we did the CT scan?''). (3) **I - Invitation**: Ask how much detail the patient wants to know. (4) **K - Knowledge**: Deliver the information in small, clear chunks without jargon. (5) **E - Empathy**: Address emotions with empathetic responses. (6) **S - Strategy & Summary**: Formulate a clear plan and summarize. (Ref: Oxford Handbook of Palliative Care; UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old male named Walter has recently undergone staging workup for progressive dyspnea and weight loss, revealing advanced glioblastoma. The primary care physician needs to deliver this diagnosis. Which of the following best describes the correct clinical application of the ''S'' (Setting) and ''P'' (Perception) steps of the SPIKES protocol for delivering bad news?', '["Ensure the patient is alone to prevent family interference, and immediately show the CT scans to explain the tumor size in detail", "Schedule the meeting in a noisy clinic waiting area to reduce the patient''s anxiety, and ask the patient''s family to step out during the disclosure", "Arrange a private, quiet setting with comfortable seating, ask the patient who else they would like to have present, and then ask open-ended questions to assess what the patient already knows or suspects about their medical condition before giving any information", "Deliver the diagnosis immediately in the hospital hallway to ensure timely communication, and skip assessing the patient''s understanding to save time", "Use highly technical medical jargon to establish clinical authority, and then ask the patient to repeat the staging classification"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The **SPIKES protocol** is a 6-step framework for delivering bad news: (1) **S - Setting**: Arrange a private, quiet environment, sit down, make eye contact, and invite key family members. (2) **P - Perception**: Assess what the patient understands about their illness using open-ended questions (e.g., ''What is your understanding of why we did the CT scan?''). (3) **I - Invitation**: Ask how much detail the patient wants to know. (4) **K - Knowledge**: Deliver the information in small, clear chunks without jargon. (5) **E - Empathy**: Address emotions with empathetic responses. (6) **S - Strategy & Summary**: Formulate a clear plan and summarize. (Ref: Oxford Handbook of Palliative Care; UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
