    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old male named Raymond has recently undergone staging workup for progressive dyspnea and weight loss, revealing metastatic colorectal cancer. The primary care physician needs to deliver this diagnosis. Which of the following best describes the correct clinical application of the ''S'' (Setting) and ''P'' (Perception) steps of the SPIKES protocol for delivering bad news?', '["Schedule the meeting in a noisy clinic waiting area to reduce the patient''s anxiety, and ask the patient''s family to step out during the disclosure", "Ensure the patient is alone to prevent family interference, and immediately show the CT scans to explain the tumor size in detail", "Use highly technical medical jargon to establish clinical authority, and then ask the patient to repeat the staging classification", "Deliver the diagnosis immediately in the hospital hallway to ensure timely communication, and skip assessing the patient''s understanding to save time", "Arrange a private, quiet setting with comfortable seating, ask the patient who else they would like to have present, and then ask open-ended questions to assess what the patient already knows or suspects about their medical condition before giving any information"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The **SPIKES protocol** is a 6-step framework for delivering bad news: (1) **S - Setting**: Arrange a private, quiet environment, sit down, make eye contact, and invite key family members. (2) **P - Perception**: Assess what the patient understands about their illness using open-ended questions (e.g., ''What is your understanding of why we did the CT scan?''). (3) **I - Invitation**: Ask how much detail the patient wants to know. (4) **K - Knowledge**: Deliver the information in small, clear chunks without jargon. (5) **E - Empathy**: Address emotions with empathetic responses. (6) **S - Strategy & Summary**: Formulate a clear plan and summarize. (Ref: Oxford Handbook of Palliative Care; UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
