ls. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 21 & 82; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old male named Stanley with advanced multi-organ dysfunction, severe frailty, and end-stage congestive heart failure is discussing cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) preferences during an annual wellness visit. What is the most accurate, evidence-based information the clinician should provide to Stanley regarding the outcomes of CPR in this clinical context?', '["CPR is highly successful in older adults, with over 50% of octogenarians returning to their baseline functional and cognitive state after cardiac arrest", "Bystander CPR is completely ineffective in older adults and should never be attempted outside of a hospital setting", "The survival rate after CPR is identical across all age groups and is unaffected by the presence of underlying terminal illnesses", "Intubation and chest compressions are routinely performed in hospice settings because they are required to qualify for Medicare coverage", "The probability of surviving to hospital discharge after in-hospital CPR is very low (typically less than 10%, and under 2% in patients with advanced multi-organ failure), and survivors often sustain rib fractures, ischemic brain injury, and severe functional decline"]'::jsonb, 4, 'In older adults, particularly those with **severe frailty, advanced dementia, or metastatic cancer**, the outcomes of CPR are very poor. The probability of **survival to hospital discharge** after in-hospital cardiac arrest is **less than 10-15%** overall, and **under 2-3%** for patients with end-stage chronic diseases. Furthermore, CPR in this population frequently causes **severe morbidity**, including rib and sternal fractures, liver/spleen lacerations, prolonged mechanical ventilation, and hypoxic-ischemic brain damage. Providing this realistic prognostic information is crucial to help patients make informed decisions about their resuscitation status. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 82; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 11; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old female named Jean with advanced multi-organ dysfunction, severe frailty, and end-stage congestive heart failure is discussing cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) preferences during an annual wellness visit. What is the most accurate, evidence-based information the clinician should provide to Jean regarding the outcomes of CPR in this clinical context?', '["The probability of surviving to hospital discharge after in-hospital CPR is very low (typically less than 10%, and under 2% in patients with advanced multi-organ failure), and survivors often sustain rib fractures, ischemic brain injury, and severe functional decline", "Bystander CPR is completely ineffective in older adults and should never be attempted outside of a hospital setting", "Intubation and chest compressions are routinely performed in hospice settings because they are required to qualify for Medicare coverage", "CPR is highly successful in older adults, with over 50% of octogenarians returning to their baseline functional and cognitive state after cardiac arrest", "The survival rate after CPR is identical across all age groups and is unaffected by the presence of underlying terminal illnesses"]'::jsonb, 0, 'In older adults, particularly those with **severe frailty, advanced dementia, or metastatic cancer**, the outcomes of CPR are very poor. The probability of **survival to hospital discharge** after in-hospital cardiac arrest is **less than 10-15%** overall, and **under 2-3%** for patients with end-stage chronic diseases. Furthermore, CPR in this population frequently causes **severe morbidity**, including rib and sternal fractures, liver/spleen lacerations, prolonged mechanical ventilation, and hypoxic-ischemic brain damage. Providing this realistic prognostic information is crucial to help patients make informed decisions about their resuscitation status. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 82; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 11; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
