    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old male named Raymond with end-stage respiratory failure due to severe COPD has decided, in conjunction with his family, to undergo withdrawal of mechanical ventilation (terminal weaning) and focus entirely on comfort. What is the most appropriate pharmacological strategy to manage the expected tachypnea, dyspnea, and air hunger during this process?', '["Start an intravenous drip of albuterol and saline to dilate the airways and increase oxygen delivery to the brainstem", "Administer high-dose intravenous furosemide and hold all sedative medications to ensure the respiratory drive remains fully stimulated", "Infuse intravenous naloxone to prevent opioid-induced respiratory depression and maintain spontaneous breathing", "Administer an intravenous infusion of morphine titrated to comfort, as opioids are the gold standard for relieving terminal dyspnea by reducing respiratory drive and the perception of air hunger", "Use deep endotracheal suctioning every 10 minutes as the primary non-pharmacological method to clear secretions and relieve dyspnea"]'::jsonb, 3, 'During the withdrawal of mechanical ventilation (terminal weaning), the primary clinical goal is to prevent **dyspnea, tachypnea, and air hunger**. **Intravenous opioids (such as morphine)** are the **first-line agents** and gold standard for this purpose. Opioids act centrally to reduce the perception of air hunger, blunt the respiratory drive, and decrease systemic vascular resistance (reducing pulmonary congestion). Benzodiazepines (e.g., midazolam or lorazepam) can be added as adjuncts to manage concurrent anxiety or agitation. Deep suctioning is painful and stimulates airway reflexes, worsening distress, and should be avoided. (Ref: Palliative Care: A Practical Guide Ch. 5; Oxford Handbook of Palliative Care; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old female named Frances with end-stage respiratory failure due to severe COPD has decided, in conjunction with her family, to undergo withdrawal of mechanical ventilation (terminal weaning) and focus entirely on comfort. What is the most appropriate pharmacological strategy to manage the expected tachypnea, dyspnea, and air hunger during this process?', '["Administer an intravenous infusion of morphine titrated to comfort, as opioids are the gold standard for relieving terminal dyspnea by reducing respiratory drive and the perception of air hunger", "Start an intravenous drip of albuterol and saline to dilate the airways and increase oxygen delivery to the brainstem", "Infuse intravenous naloxone to prevent opioid-induced respiratory depression and maintain spontaneous breathing", "Use deep endotracheal suctioning every 10 minutes as the primary non-pharmacological method to clear secretions and relieve dyspnea", "Administer high-dose intravenous furosemide and hold all sedative medications to ensure the respiratory drive remains fully stimulated"]'::jsonb, 0, 'During the withdrawal of mechanical ventilation (terminal weaning), the primary clinical goal is to prevent **dyspnea, tachypnea, and air hunger**. **Intravenous opioids (such as morphine)** are the **first-line agents** and gold standard for this purpose. Opioids act centrally to reduce the perception of air hunger, blunt the respiratory drive, and decrease systemic vascular resistance (reducing pulmonary congestion). Benzodiazepines (e.g., midazolam or lorazepam) can be added as adjuncts to manage concurrent anxiety or agitation. Deep suctioning is painful and stimulates airway reflexes, worsening distress, and should be avoided. (Ref: Palliative Care: A Practical Guide Ch. 5; Oxford Handbook of Palliative Care; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
