    (v_topic_id, 'A 84-year-old male named Harry with end-stage respiratory failure due to severe COPD has decided, in conjunction with his family, to undergo withdrawal of mechanical ventilation (terminal weaning) and focus entirely on comfort. What is the most appropriate pharmacological strategy to manage the expected tachypnea, dyspnea, and air hunger during this process?', '["Administer an intravenous infusion of morphine titrated to comfort, as opioids are the gold standard for relieving terminal dyspnea by reducing respiratory drive and the perception of air hunger", "Infuse intravenous naloxone to prevent opioid-induced respiratory depression and maintain spontaneous breathing", "Administer high-dose intravenous furosemide and hold all sedative medications to ensure the respiratory drive remains fully stimulated", "Use deep endotracheal suctioning every 10 minutes as the primary non-pharmacological method to clear secretions and relieve dyspnea", "Start an intravenous drip of albuterol and saline to dilate the airways and increase oxygen delivery to the brainstem"]'::jsonb, 0, 'During the withdrawal of mechanical ventilation (terminal weaning), the primary clinical goal is to prevent **dyspnea, tachypnea, and air hunger**. **Intravenous opioids (such as morphine)** are the **first-line agents** and gold standard for this purpose. Opioids act centrally to reduce the perception of air hunger, blunt the respiratory drive, and decrease systemic vascular resistance (reducing pulmonary congestion). Benzodiazepines (e.g., midazolam or lorazepam) can be added as adjuncts to manage concurrent anxiety or agitation. Deep suctioning is painful and stimulates airway reflexes, worsening distress, and should be avoided. (Ref: Palliative Care: A Practical Guide Ch. 5; Oxford Handbook of Palliative Care; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old male named Herbert with inoperable, recurrent peritoneal carcinomatosis from metastatic colorectal cancer is admitted with a malignant bowel obstruction. He has severe, crampy abdominal pain, distension, and frequent vomiting of feculent material. Surgery is ruled out. Which of the following pharmacological regimens is most appropriate to manage his symptoms?', '["Prescribe oral metoclopramide at high doses to stimulate gastric emptying and force the bowel obstruction to clear", "Initiate a aggressive bowel regimen with oral senna, docusate, and polyethylene glycol to dissolve the obstruction", "Initiate intravenous octreotide to reduce gastrointestinal secretions and peristalsis, and intravenous dexamethasone to decrease tumor-associated edema; avoid prokinetics like metoclopramide", "Administer a continuous intravenous infusion of loperamide to stop the vomiting by halting all intestinal motility", "Insert a nasogastric tube for permanent decompression and advise that no medications can manage these symptoms"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Malignant bowel obstruction in advanced, inoperable cancer is managed medically with a focus on symptom control: (1) **Octreotide (somatostatin analog)** is highly effective; it reduces gastrointestinal secretions, splanchnic blood flow, and peristalsis, thereby relieving cramping pain, nausea, and vomiting. (2) **Corticosteroids (dexamethasone)** reduce tumor-associated inflammation and edema, which may partially restore patency. (3) **Anticholinergics (e.g., hyoscyamine, glycopyrrolate)** reduce colic. **Prokinetics like metoclopramide are contraindicated** in complete mechanical obstruction as they increase colic and risk bowel perforation. (Ref: Oxford Handbook of Palliative Care; Hazzard''s Ch. 82; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old female named Florence with inoperable, recurrent peritoneal carcinomatosis from advanced glioblastoma is admitted with a malignant bowel obstruction. She has severe, crampy abdominal pain, distension, and frequent vomiting of feculent material. Surgery is ruled out. Which of the following pharmacological regimens is most appropriate to manage her symptoms?', '["Prescribe oral metoclopramide at high doses to stimulate gastric emptying and force the bowel obstruction to clear", "Administer a continuous intravenous infusion of loperamide to stop the vomiting by halting all intestinal motility", "Initiate a aggressive bowel regimen with oral senna, docusate, and polyethylene glycol to dissolve the obstruction", "Initiate intravenous octreotide to reduce gastrointestinal secretions and peristalsis, and intravenous dexamethasone to decrease tumor-associated edema; avoid prokinetics like metoclopramide", "Insert a nasogastric tube for permanent decompression and advise that no medications can manage these symptoms"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Malignant bowel obstruction in advanced, inoperable cancer is managed medically with a focus on symptom control: (1) **Octreotide (somatostatin analog)** is highly effective; it reduces gastrointestinal secretions, splanchnic blood flow, and peristalsis, thereby relieving cramping pain, nausea, and vomiting. (2) **Corticosteroids (dexamethasone)** reduce tumor-associated inflammation and edema, which may partially restore patency. (3) **Anticholinergics (e.g., hyoscyamine, glycopyrrolate)** reduce colic. **Prokinetics like metoclopramide are contraindicated** in complete mechanical obstruction as they increase colic and risk bowel perforation. (Ref: Oxford Handbook of Palliative Care; Hazzard''s Ch. 82; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 80-year-old male named Harry with inoperable, recurrent peritoneal carcinomatosis from advanced metastatic breast cancer is admitted with a malignant bowel obstruction. He has severe, crampy abdominal pain, distension, and frequent vomiting of feculent material. Surgery is ruled out. Which of the following pharmacological regimens is most appropriate to manage his symptoms?', '["Insert a nasogastric tube for permanent decompression and advise that no medications can manage these symptoms", "Administer a continuous intravenous infusion of loperamide to stop the vomiting by halting all intestinal motility", "Prescribe oral metoclopramide at high doses to stimulate gastric emptying and force the bowel obstruction to clear", "Initiate a aggressive bowel regimen with oral senna, docusate, and polyethylene glycol to dissolve the obstruction", "Initiate intravenous octreotide to reduce gastrointestinal secretions and peristalsis, and intravenous dexamethasone to decrease tumor-associated edema; avoid prokinetics like metoclopramide"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Malignant bowel obstruction in advanced, inoperable cancer is managed medically with a focus on symptom control: (1) **Octreotide (somatostatin analog)** is highly effective; it reduces gastrointestinal secretions, splanchnic blood flow, and peristalsis, thereby relieving cramping pain, nausea, and vomiting. (2) **Corticosteroids (dexamethasone)** reduce tumor-associated inflammation and edema, which may partially restore patency. (3) **Anticholinergics (e.g., hyoscyamine, glycopyrrolate)** reduce colic. **Prokinetics like metoclopramide are contraindicated** in complete mechanical obstruction as they increase colic and risk bowel perforation. (Ref: Oxford Handbook of Palliative Care; Hazzard''s Ch. 82; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
