    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old male named William with NYHA Class IV ischemic cardiomyopathy has had 3 hospitalizations for decompensated heart failure in the past 8 months. Despite optimal medical therapy (beta-blocker, ACE inhibitor, mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist, and SGLT2 inhibitor), he has dyspnea at rest and is increasingly fatigued. Which of the following is the most appropriate communication strategy regarding prognosis and goals of care?', '["Initiate a proactive, shared decision-making discussion to review the progressive nature of end-stage heart failure, explain the poor 1-year prognosis, and explore his values regarding future therapies (e.g., home inotropes, ICD deactivation, or hospice care)", "Avoid discussing prognosis to prevent taking away the patient''s hope, and reassure he that his heart failure will completely resolve with a new medication dose", "Tell the patient that because they have heart failure instead of cancer, they are ineligible for any palliative care or hospice services", "Unilaterally deactivate the patient''s implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD) during the clinic visit without discussing it with the patient or family", "Advise the patient that they must immediately receive a left ventricular assist device (LVAD) or heart transplant, as there are no comfort-focused options for heart failure"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Advanced heart failure (NYHA Class IV) carries a poor prognosis (approx. 50% 1-year mortality). Clinicians should initiate **proactive, shared decision-making discussions** about: (1) The progressive nature of the disease, (2) Expected prognosis, (3) Preferences for life-sustaining treatments (e.g., intubation, chest compressions), (4) **ICD deactivation** (to prevent painful shocks during dying), (5) **Palliative treatments** (e.g., low-dose opioids for dyspnea, home inotropic support), and (6) **Hospice eligibility**. Avoiding these discussions leads to crisis-driven, non-aligned ICU interventions. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 39 & 82; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
