    (v_topic_id, 'A 90-year-old female named Gertrude with NYHA Class IV ischemic cardiomyopathy has had 3 hospitalizations for decompensated heart failure in the past 6 months. Despite optimal medical therapy (beta-blocker, ACE inhibitor, mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist, and SGLT2 inhibitor), she has dyspnea at rest and is increasingly fatigued. Which of the following is the most appropriate communication strategy regarding prognosis and goals of care?', '["Unilaterally deactivate the patient''s implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD) during the clinic visit without discussing it with the patient or family", "Tell the patient that because they have heart failure instead of cancer, they are ineligible for any palliative care or hospice services", "Avoid discussing prognosis to prevent taking away the patient''s hope, and reassure she that her heart failure will completely resolve with a new medication dose", "Advise the patient that they must immediately receive a left ventricular assist device (LVAD) or heart transplant, as there are no comfort-focused options for heart failure", "Initiate a proactive, shared decision-making discussion to review the progressive nature of end-stage heart failure, explain the poor 1-year prognosis, and explore her values regarding future therapies (e.g., home inotropes, ICD deactivation, or hospice care)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Advanced heart failure (NYHA Class IV) carries a poor prognosis (approx. 50% 1-year mortality). Clinicians should initiate **proactive, shared decision-making discussions** about: (1) The progressive nature of the disease, (2) Expected prognosis, (3) Preferences for life-sustaining treatments (e.g., intubation, chest compressions), (4) **ICD deactivation** (to prevent painful shocks during dying), (5) **Palliative treatments** (e.g., low-dose opioids for dyspnea, home inotropic support), and (6) **Hospice eligibility**. Avoiding these discussions leads to crisis-driven, non-aligned ICU interventions. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 39 & 82; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 96-year-old male named David is admitted with a massive hemorrhagic stroke and is unable to communicate. He has a valid Living Will stating he does ''not want mechanical ventilation or artificial life support under any circumstances.'' However, his designated Durable Power of Attorney for Healthcare (DPOA-HC), his son, is highly emotional and insists that the medical team intubate the patient and place he on a ventilator. Which of the following is the correct ethical and legal priority?', '["The DPOA-HC has absolute authority to override any written documents, so the medical team must immediately intubate the patient", "The physician should perform the intubation but secretly plan to extubate the patient once the surrogate leaves the hospital", "The Living Will represents the patient''s autonomous wishes and takes legal precedence over the surrogate''s contradictory request; the physician must explain this to the surrogate, refuse intubation, and focus on comfort-focused care", "The living will is only legally binding if it was signed in the presence of a judge, so it should be ignored in the acute hospital setting", "The medical team should refer the case to the state medical board to decide, maintaining mechanical ventilation in the interim"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A **Living Will** is a legal document that outlines a patient''s specific medical preferences (e.g., refusing intubation, feeding tubes) if they lose decision-making capacity and have a terminal condition or persistent vegetative state. A **Durable Power of Attorney for Healthcare (DPOA-HC)** designates a surrogate decision-maker. Ethically and legally, **the surrogate''s role is to enforce the patient''s known wishes (substituted judgment)**, not their own desires. If a surrogate requests an intervention (e.g., intubation) that directly contradicts a clear, specific directive in the patient''s valid Living Will, the **Living Will takes precedence**. The clinician should counsel the surrogate, review the document together, and align the care plan with the patient''s documented wishes. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 21; UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 94-year-old male named Richard is admitted with a massive hemorrhagic stroke and is unable to communicate. He has a valid Living Will stating he does ''not want mechanical ventilation or artificial life support under any circumstances.'' However, his designated Durable Power of Attorney for Healthcare (DPOA-HC), his brother, is highly emotional and insists that the medical team intubate the patient and place he on a ventilator. Which of the following is the correct ethical and legal priority?', '["The medical team should refer the case to the state medical board to decide, maintaining mechanical ventilation in the interim", "The DPOA-HC has absolute authority to override any written documents, so the medical team must immediately intubate the patient", "The Living Will represents the patient''s autonomous wishes and takes legal precedence over the surrogate''s contradictory request; the physician must explain this to the surrogate, refuse intubation, and focus on comfort-focused care", "The living will is only legally binding if it was signed in the presence of a judge, so it should be ignored in the acute hospital setting", "The physician should perform the intubation but secretly plan to extubate the patient once the surrogate leaves the hospital"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A **Living Will** is a legal document that outlines a patient''s specific medical preferences (e.g., refusing intubation, feeding tubes) if they lose decision-making capacity and have a terminal condition or persistent vegetative state. A **Durable Power of Attorney for Healthcare (DPOA-HC)** designates a surrogate decision-maker. Ethically and legally, **the surrogate''s role is to enforce the patient''s known wishes (substituted judgment)**, not their own desires. If a surrogate requests an intervention (e.g., intubation) that directly contradicts a clear, specific directive in the patient''s valid Living Will, the **Living Will takes precedence**. The clinician should counsel the surrogate, review the document together, and align the care plan with the patient''s documented wishes. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 21; UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
