    (v_topic_id, 'A 92-year-old male named Harry is admitted with a massive hemorrhagic stroke and is unable to communicate. He has a valid Living Will stating he does ''not want mechanical ventilation or artificial life support under any circumstances.'' However, his designated Durable Power of Attorney for Healthcare (DPOA-HC), his sister, is highly emotional and insists that the medical team intubate the patient and place he on a ventilator. Which of the following is the correct ethical and legal priority?', '["The physician should perform the intubation but secretly plan to extubate the patient once the surrogate leaves the hospital", "The DPOA-HC has absolute authority to override any written documents, so the medical team must immediately intubate the patient", "The Living Will represents the patient''s autonomous wishes and takes legal precedence over the surrogate''s contradictory request; the physician must explain this to the surrogate, refuse intubation, and focus on comfort-focused care", "The medical team should refer the case to the state medical board to decide, maintaining mechanical ventilation in the interim", "The living will is only legally binding if it was signed in the presence of a judge, so it should be ignored in the acute hospital setting"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A **Living Will** is a legal document that outlines a patient''s specific medical preferences (e.g., refusing intubation, feeding tubes) if they lose decision-making capacity and have a terminal condition or persistent vegetative state. A **Durable Power of Attorney for Healthcare (DPOA-HC)** designates a surrogate decision-maker. Ethically and legally, **the surrogate''s role is to enforce the patient''s known wishes (substituted judgment)**, not their own desires. If a surrogate requests an intervention (e.g., intubation) that directly contradicts a clear, specific directive in the patient''s valid Living Will, the **Living Will takes precedence**. The clinician should counsel the surrogate, review the document together, and align the care plan with the patient''s documented wishes. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 21; UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old female named Irene is admitted with a massive hemorrhagic stroke and is unable to communicate. She has a valid Living Will stating she does ''not want mechanical ventilation or artificial life support under any circumstances.'' However, her designated Durable Power of Attorney for Healthcare (DPOA-HC), her daughter, is highly emotional and insists that the medical team intubate the patient and place she on a ventilator. Which of the following is the correct ethical and legal priority?', '["The Living Will represents the patient''s autonomous wishes and takes legal precedence over the surrogate''s contradictory request; the physician must explain this to the surrogate, refuse intubation, and focus on comfort-focused care", "The medical team should refer the case to the state medical board to decide, maintaining mechanical ventilation in the interim", "The physician should perform the intubation but secretly plan to extubate the patient once the surrogate leaves the hospital", "The living will is only legally binding if it was signed in the presence of a judge, so it should be ignored in the acute hospital setting", "The DPOA-HC has absolute authority to override any written documents, so the medical team must immediately intubate the patient"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A **Living Will** is a legal document that outlines a patient''s specific medical preferences (e.g., refusing intubation, feeding tubes) if they lose decision-making capacity and have a terminal condition or persistent vegetative state. A **Durable Power of Attorney for Healthcare (DPOA-HC)** designates a surrogate decision-maker. Ethically and legally, **the surrogate''s role is to enforce the patient''s known wishes (substituted judgment)**, not their own desires. If a surrogate requests an intervention (e.g., intubation) that directly contradicts a clear, specific directive in the patient''s valid Living Will, the **Living Will takes precedence**. The clinician should counsel the surrogate, review the document together, and align the care plan with the patient''s documented wishes. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 21; UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
