gate, review the document together, and align the care plan with the patient''s documented wishes. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 21; UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old male named Harry is admitted with a massive hemorrhagic stroke and is unable to communicate. He has a valid Living Will stating he does ''not want mechanical ventilation or artificial life support under any circumstances.'' However, his designated Durable Power of Attorney for Healthcare (DPOA-HC), his spouse, is highly emotional and insists that the medical team intubate the patient and place he on a ventilator. Which of the following is the correct ethical and legal priority?', '["The Living Will represents the patient''s autonomous wishes and takes legal precedence over the surrogate''s contradictory request; the physician must explain this to the surrogate, refuse intubation, and focus on comfort-focused care", "The physician should perform the intubation but secretly plan to extubate the patient once the surrogate leaves the hospital", "The DPOA-HC has absolute authority to override any written documents, so the medical team must immediately intubate the patient", "The medical team should refer the case to the state medical board to decide, maintaining mechanical ventilation in the interim", "The living will is only legally binding if it was signed in the presence of a judge, so it should be ignored in the acute hospital setting"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A **Living Will** is a legal document that outlines a patient''s specific medical preferences (e.g., refusing intubation, feeding tubes) if they lose decision-making capacity and have a terminal condition or persistent vegetative state. A **Durable Power of Attorney for Healthcare (DPOA-HC)** designates a surrogate decision-maker. Ethically and legally, **the surrogate''s role is to enforce the patient''s known wishes (substituted judgment)**, not their own desires. If a surrogate requests an intervention (e.g., intubation) that directly contradicts a clear, specific directive in the patient''s valid Living Will, the **Living Will takes precedence**. The clinician should counsel the surrogate, review the document together, and align the care plan with the patient''s documented wishes. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 21; UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old male named Roy is transitioning from an acute care hospital to a long-term care facility. His physician recommends completing a Physician Orders for Life-Sustaining Treatment (POLST) form. How does a POLST/MOLST differ from a standard Living Will?', '["A POLST is a set of actionable medical orders signed by a clinician that is immediately binding across all care settings (including emergency services), whereas a Living Will is a legal document expressing general wishes that requires translation into medical orders", "A POLST is only valid inside a courtroom and has no authority in a nursing home or hospital setting", "A POLST only addresses organ donation, while a Living Will addresses all cardiopulmonary resuscitation preferences", "A POLST requires the patient to name a healthcare proxy, whereas a Living Will is designed to assign power of attorney", "A Living Will is a set of binding medical orders signed by a physician, while a POLST is a non-binding legal draft written by an attorney"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The **POLST (Physician Orders for Life-Sustaining Treatment)** or **MOLST (Medical Orders for Life-Sustaining Treatment)** is a clinical tool designed for patients with serious illness or frailty. Unlike a **Living Will** (which is a legal document describing future preferences and requires translation into orders), a POLST: (1) Consists of **actionable medical orders** signed by a physician, nurse practitioner, or physician assistant, (2) Is **immediately binding across all settings**, including by emergency medical services (EMS) in the home, (3) Focuses on current medical decisions (resuscitation, intubation, feeding tubes, antibiotics). A living will is often not accessible or immediately actionable by EMS. (Ref: AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Hazzard''s Ch. 82; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 92-year-old male named Eugene is transitioning from an acute care hospital to a long-term care facility. His physician recommends completing a Physician Orders for Life-Sustaining Treatment (POLST) form. How does a POLST/MOLST differ from a standard Living Will?', '["A POLST requires the patient to name a healthcare proxy, whereas a Living Will is designed to assign power of attorney", "A POLST is only valid inside a courtroom and has no authority in a nursing home or hospital setting", "A POLST only addresses organ donation, while a Living Will addresses all cardiopulmonary resuscitation preferences", "A Living Will is a set of binding medical orders signed by a physician, while a POLST is a non-binding legal draft written by an attorney", "A POLST is a set of actionable medical orders signed by a clinician that is immediately binding across all care settings (including emergency services), whereas a Living Will is a legal document expressing general wishes that requires translation into medical orders"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The **POLST (Physician Orders for Life-Sustaining Treatment)** or **MOLST (Medical Orders for Life-Sustaining Treatment)** is a clinical tool designed for patients with serious illness or frailty. Unlike a **Living Will** (which is a legal document describing future preferences and requires translation into orders), a POLST: (1) Consists of **actionable medical orders** signed by a physician, nurse practitioner, or physician assistant, (2) Is **immediately binding across all settings**, including by emergency medical services (EMS) in the home, (3) Focuses on current medical decisions (resuscitation, intubation, feeding tubes, antibiotics). A living will is often not accessible or immediately actionable by EMS. (Ref: AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Hazzard''s Ch. 82; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
